# SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.Universal — Community Edition (free, cross-platform)

Expert guidance for **SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.Universal**, the free cross-platform HTML to PDF converter:
Windows (x64, x86), Linux (x64, ARM64), macOS on Apple Silicon and Docker, rendering with Chromium.
HTML→PDF only, **5 pages per document**. Current as of **v26.5**.

## Pick the right rule first

- **Free cross-platform Community Edition (this rule)** — `SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.Universal` +
  `SelectPdf.Universal.Native.<rid>` (or one `SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.<Platform>` package); code uses
  `using SelectPdf.Universal;`.
- **Full cross-platform library** — documents over 5 pages, HTML to image, PDF creation or editing, Word to
  PDF, tables of contents with page numbers, HTML forms to fillable fields, forms, signatures, redaction,
  tagged PDF/UA, PDF/A level A or e-invoices → **`selectpdf-lib-universal-net`**.
  It uses the same namespace and the same code, so moving up is a package swap.
- **Classic Windows Community Edition** — `Select.HtmlToPdf` / `Select.HtmlToPdf.NetCore`, code uses
  `using SelectPdf;` → **`selectpdf-lib-htmltopdf-net`**. To port classic code, read
  [`migrating-from-classic.md`](https://github.com/selectpdf/ai-tools/blob/main/plugins/selectpdf/skills/selectpdf-lib-htmltopdf-universal-net/reference/migrating-from-classic.md).
- **Online REST API** — the project calls `api.selectpdf.com` over HTTP → a `selectpdf-api-*` skill.

## Install

For an application that runs on one platform, one package installs the converter and that platform's engine:

```bash
dotnet add package SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.Linux
```

`SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.Windows` (x64), `SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.Linux` (x64, Docker), `SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.Linux.Arm64`,
`SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.MacOS` (Apple Silicon, macOS 13 or newer). Or install the two packages they bring in: the
converter and the native engine for the runtime you deploy to:

```bash
dotnet add package SelectPdf.HtmlToPdf.Universal
dotnet add package SelectPdf.Universal.Native.linux-x64
```

Native packages: `win-x64`, `win-x86`, `linux-x64`, `linux-arm64`, `osx-arm64`, the same ones the full library
uses. Pick the one matching the **architecture of the process that converts**. **A project references one
native package**; to publish for several platforms, choose it with a `Condition` on `$(RuntimeIdentifier)` and
publish once per platform. The library targets .NET 8, .NET 10 and .NET Standard 2.0 (usable from .NET
Framework 4.6.1+). Optional: `SelectPdf.Universal.Fonts` adds Noto fonts for every script, for Linux hosts and
containers with no fonts.

**Remote engine:** with `GlobalProperties.RenderingEngine = RenderingEngine.ChromiumRemote` and
`GlobalProperties.EngineEndpoint` (plus `EngineAccessToken`), conversions run in the `ghcr.io/selectpdf/engine`
container and the app needs no native package. This is the supported way to run on **Alpine / musl** Linux. See
[`installation-and-deployment.md`](https://github.com/selectpdf/ai-tools/blob/main/plugins/selectpdf/skills/selectpdf-lib-htmltopdf-universal-net/reference/installation-and-deployment.md), "The remote engine".

> **Platform limits for the native engines:** glibc-based Linux only (glibc 2.30+). No HTML-to-PDF on Intel
> Macs (no `osx-x64` engine), and no Windows-on-ARM engine. Elsewhere, use the remote engine.

## License

Free for personal and commercial use; no license key is needed, and `GlobalProperties` has no `LicenseKey`
property in this edition. The only limit is **5 pages per document**.

## Quickstart

```csharp
using SelectPdf.Universal;

// the same code on Windows, Linux and macOS
HtmlToPdf converter = new HtmlToPdf();
using PdfDocument doc = converter.ConvertUrl("https://selectpdf.com");
doc.Save("output.pdf");
```
```vbnet
Imports SelectPdf.Universal

' the same code on Windows, Linux and macOS
Dim converter As New HtmlToPdf()
Using doc As PdfDocument = converter.ConvertUrl("https://selectpdf.com")
    doc.Save("output.pdf")
End Using
```

`ConvertHtmlString(html, baseUrl)` converts an HTML string. `ConvertUrlAsync` / `ConvertHtmlStringAsync`
(optionally with a `CancellationToken`) convert without holding a thread; `doc.Save()` with no arguments
returns a `byte[]`. `converter.ConversionResult.PdfPageCountBeforeLimit` is the number of pages the content
produced before the 5-page limit: when it is larger than `PdfPageCount`, the limit cut the document.

Rules that always apply:
- `using SelectPdf.Universal;` — **no `System.Drawing`** in the API.
- **Chromium is the only engine**; never set `WebKit`, `WebKitRestricted` or `Blink`.
- Always `Close()` documents, or put them in a `using` statement (`PdfDocument` is `IDisposable`). One
  converter per conversion; conversions running at once are limited by
  `HtmlToPdfOptions.MaximumConcurrentConversions` (default 8).

